        <img src={logoPath} width="900" height="900" alt="" />
        <span>DMI · Calamba City</span>
      </a>
      <LanguageToggle translations={translations} />
      <span>27 September 2026</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="study-hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="eyebrow">Bible Study · Genesis 26:17–33</p>
      <h1 data-i18n="title">Dig Deep in God’s Presence</h1>
      <p class="study-subtitle" data-i18n="subtitle">Contending for Jesus in Our Lives</p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="hero-scripture">
      <p lang="en" data-i18n="heroScripture" data-i18n-lang><sup>17</sup> So Isaac departed from there and encamped in the Valley of Gerar and settled there. <sup>18</sup> And Isaac dug again the wells of water that they had dug in the days of Abraham his father, which the Philistines had stopped after the death of Abraham. And he gave them the names that his father had given them. <sup>19</sup> But when Isaac’s servants dug in the valley and found there a well of spring water, <sup>20</sup> the herdsmen of Gerar quarreled with Isaac’s herdsmen, saying, “The water is ours.” So he called the name of the well Esek, because they contended with him. <sup>21</sup> Then they dug another well, and they quarreled over that also, so he called its name Sitnah. <sup>22</sup> And he moved from there and dug another well, and they did not quarrel over it. So he called its name Rehoboth, saying, “For now the Lord has made room for us, and we shall be fruitful in the land.”</p>
      <cite lang="en" data-i18n="heroCitation" data-i18n-lang>Genesis 26:17–22 · ESV</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </header>

  <main class="study-content">
    <aside class="study-aside" aria-label="Study summary" data-i18n-aria-label="summaryAria">
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="themeLabel">Theme</span><p data-i18n="theme">Because Jesus is the true source of life, we keep clearing and deepening our access to him until his living water overflows through our lives and community.</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="keyTextsLabel">Key texts</span><p data-i18n="keyTexts">Genesis 26:17–33<br />Psalm 42:1<br />Philippians 4:6–7<br />1 Corinthians 10:13<br />John 7:37–38</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="timeLabel">Time</span><p data-i18n="time">35–40 minutes</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="presentationLabel">Presentation</span><a class="aside-action" href="https://www.canva.com/design/DAHWUYqvnOE/91F1TA1Fwpn4ALALRl8-jA/edit" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="presentationLink">Open in Canva <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="serviceLabel">Sunday service</span><p data-i18n="serviceUnavailable">No separate YouTube link was provided</p></div>
      <div><span class="aside-label" data-i18n="facebookLabel">Facebook Live</span><a class="aside-action" href="https://www.facebook.com/DMICalamba/videos/1766143824591506" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="facebookLink">Watch on Facebook <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    </aside>

    <article class="study-article">
      <section class="opening" aria-labelledby="opening-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section01">01 · Begin at the well</p>
        <h2 id="opening-title" data-i18n="openingTitle">We cannot survive apart from the Source</h2>
        <p class="lead" data-i18n="openingLead">Isaac was a sojourner in Gerar. He was pushed away from the center, yet wherever he went he dug—and reopened wells that had been buried after Abraham died.</p>
        <p data-i18n="openingBody1">A well was not a luxury for Isaac’s household, servants, and livestock. It was their access to the water they needed to live. The picture reaches into our spiritual life: the well is not the source; it is the place where we continually draw from God.</p>
        <p data-i18n="openingBody2">Jesus himself is the life-giving water. Personal prayer, reading, meditating on, memorizing, and obeying Scripture are practices that open our lives to him. No matter how long we have followed Christ, we cannot grow on yesterday’s water alone. We must keep coming to the Source.</p>
        <div class="key-statement"><span data-i18n="keyTruthLabel">Key truth</span><p data-i18n="keyTruth">We are not contending for Jesus as though he needs our defense; we are contending for his place in our hearts against everything that would displace him.</p></div>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss1Label">Discuss · 1</span><p data-i18n="discuss1">What does the picture of a well teach us about dependence on Jesus? Where are you presently drawing strength, satisfaction, and direction in practice?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="buried-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section02">02 · Clear what has buried the well</p>
        <h2 id="buried-title" data-i18n="buriedTitle">Hunger for God digs beneath distraction</h2>
        <p data-i18n="buriedBody1">Psalm 42 gives us the language of intense desire: as a deer longs for flowing water, the soul longs for God. This is not casual interest. It is a heart saying, “Lord, I cannot live without You.”</p>
        <p data-i18n="buriedBody2">Yet many things can bury our access to Jesus: problems we rehearse, sin we refuse to flee, worldly desires, constant busyness, ideologies we hear more readily than God’s Word, and the endless noise of social media and entertainment.</p>
        <p data-i18n="buriedBody3">When anxious, we are invited to pray with thanksgiving and receive God’s peace. When tempted, he provides a way of escape that we must take. When confused, his Word lights our path. Digging is not escaping our problems; it is bringing our whole life back to Jesus.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card"><span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · study translation</span><p lang="en" data-i18n="hungerVerse" data-i18n-lang>“As a deer pants for flowing streams, so pants my soul for you, O God.”</p><cite lang="en" data-i18n="hungerCitation" data-i18n-lang>Psalm 42:1 · ESV</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss2Label">Discuss · 2</span><p data-i18n="discuss2">What “soil” is presently covering your well—anxiety, sin, busyness, false voices, or distraction? What is one concrete way to clear it this week?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="opposition-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section03">03 · Keep digging through opposition</p>
        <h2 id="opposition-title" data-i18n="oppositionTitle">Esek and Sitnah do not have the final word</h2>
        <p data-i18n="oppositionBody1">Isaac named the first disputed well <em>Esek</em>—contention. He named the next one <em>Sitnah</em>—hostility, opposition, and accusation. At times, others may claim our work, or faithful obedience may be met with conflict.</p>
        <p data-i18n="oppositionBody2">Isaac did not stop, and he did not make fighting the center of his life. He kept looking for water. In the same way, opposition is not a reason to abandon prayer, Scripture, worship, and fellowship. It is a season to hold more deeply to the true Source.</p>
        <p data-i18n="oppositionBody3">The preacher recalled a moment when he did not assert his rights before an exhausted, harsh police officer after a road accident. This does not mean we never defend what is right; wisdom is essential. But there are times when our witness carries more weight than winning an argument.</p>
        <div class="key-statement"><span data-i18n="keyTruthLabel">Key truth</span><p data-i18n="keyTruth2">Keep digging—especially when opposition comes.</p></div>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss3Label">Discuss · 3</span><p data-i18n="discuss3">In a conflict you are facing, what are you guarding most—being proved right or the witness of Jesus? What might wisdom, truth, and restraint look like there?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="provision-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section04">04 · Receive the room God provides</p>
        <h2 id="provision-title" data-i18n="provisionTitle">At Rehoboth, God receives the credit</h2>
        <p data-i18n="provisionBody1">No one quarreled over the third well. Isaac called it <em>Rehoboth</em>—a broad place or spaciousness. He did not say, “Look what I accomplished.” He said, “The Lord has made room for us.” God is the Provider; the well is his grace.</p>
        <p data-i18n="provisionBody2">We can act like children surrounded by toys yet fighting over the one in another child’s hands when we believe grace is scarce. We compare, envy, or imitate another person’s calling. But God’s grace is spacious. We do not need to grasp for another person’s gift, ministry, or recognition.</p>
        <p data-i18n="provisionBody3">Each of us is called to dig deeply and connect to the same Source. Christ’s grace is sufficient to form the calling he has entrusted to each person and to make us fruitful where he has placed us.</p>
        <div class="key-statement"><span data-i18n="keyTruthLabel">Key truth</span><p data-i18n="keyTruth3">We do not need to fight over God’s blessing. He has room for every life rooted in Christ.</p></div>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss4Label">Discuss · 4</span><p data-i18n="discuss4">Where are you tempted to compare your calling or gifting with someone else’s? How does Rehoboth invite you to give thanks, dig, and be faithful in the room God has given you?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="beersheba-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section05">05 · Put encounter before the next excavation</p>
        <h2 id="beersheba-title" data-i18n="beershebaTitle">Altar, tent, and well</h2>
        <p data-i18n="beershebaBody1">At Beersheba, the Lord first appeared to Isaac and said, “Fear not, for I am with you.” Isaac responded by building an altar, calling on the Lord’s name, and pitching his tent. Then his servants dug again.</p>
        <p data-i18n="beershebaBody2">The order matters: encounter, worship, settled presence, and then work. We do not dig to force God to provide. We dig in response to the God who has drawn near, spoken, and promised to be with us.</p>
        <p data-i18n="beershebaBody3">The name Beersheba joins the well with an oath. For us, the sure covenant does not rest on our good works but on God’s grace received through faith in Jesus. We have access to the Source because Christ opened the way.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card"><span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · study translation</span><p lang="en" data-i18n="altarVerse" data-i18n-lang><sup>24</sup> “I am the God of Abraham your father. Fear not, for I am with you and will bless you.” <sup>25</sup> So he built an altar there and called upon the name of the Lord and pitched his tent there. And there Isaac’s servants dug a well.</p><cite lang="en" data-i18n="altarCitation" data-i18n-lang>Genesis 26:24–25 · ESV</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss5Label">Discuss · 5</span><p data-i18n="discuss5">What usually comes first in your life—striving or encounter with God? What rhythm of altar, tent, and well would help you live from grace instead of trying to earn it?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="overflow-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section06">06 · Let the well become an overflow</p>
        <h2 id="overflow-title" data-i18n="overflowTitle">Living water does not end with us</h2>
        <p data-i18n="overflowBody1">The sermon recounted the Moravian community—Christian refugees divided by cultural and doctrinal differences. Instead of giving up on one another, they sought God through prayer, Bible study, repentance, and a renewed commitment to love. Conflict gave way to reconciliation and shared prayer.</p>
        <p data-i18n="overflowBody2">Their renewal did not end with them. It reached children, communities, and mission beyond their home. This is the shape of “dig deep in His presence, into the overflow”: as we reconnect to Jesus, his life in us becomes blessing for others.</p>
        <p data-i18n="overflowBody3">Jesus does not merely promise enough water to help us survive. He invites the thirsty to come and drink, then promises rivers of living water flowing from those who believe. A personal well is meant to become shared prayer, reconciliation, service, and mission.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card"><span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · study translation</span><p lang="en" data-i18n="livingWaterVerse" data-i18n-lang><sup>37</sup> “If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. <sup>38</sup> Whoever believes in me, as the Scripture has said, ‘Out of his heart will flow rivers of living water.’”</p><cite lang="en" data-i18n="livingWaterCitation" data-i18n-lang>John 7:37–38 · ESV</cite></blockquote>
        <div class="discussion-prompt"><span data-i18n="discuss6Label">Discuss · 6</span><p data-i18n="discuss6">What relationship or group needs repentance, reconciliation, and shared prayer? Who might be reached by the overflow if you began drawing from Jesus together again?</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="response-section" aria-labelledby="response-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section07">07 · Respond</p>
        <h2 id="response-title" data-i18n="responseTitle">Clear the debris. Come to the Source. Keep digging.</h2>
        <p data-i18n="responseBody1">Jesus calls us to come thirsty, not to pretend that we are self-sufficient. We can name what has buried the well and bring it to him with repentance, trust, and confidence that he will help us.</p>
        <p data-i18n="responseBody2">Digging does not have to remain private. Invite others into a rhythm of prayer and Scripture, repair what needs reconciliation, and let the grace you receive flow toward your home, church, school, workplace, and city.</p>
        <ol class="response-steps">
          <li><span data-i18n="responseStepLabel">Respond</span><p data-i18n="responseClear">Name and surrender to Jesus one thing that has buried your well.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="responseStepLabel">Respond</span><p data-i18n="responseDraw">Set a daily time this week for prayer, Scripture, meditation, and obedience.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="responseStepLabel">Respond</span><p data-i18n="responseWitness">In one conflict, choose truth and a step that protects the witness of Jesus.</p></li>
          <li><span data-i18n="responseStepLabel">Respond</span><p data-i18n="responseOverflow">Invite two or three people into regular prayer and one concrete act of reconciliation, service, or mission.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="key-statement dark"><span data-i18n="promiseLabel">The promise</span><p data-i18n="promise">“If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. Whoever believes in me… ‘Out of his heart will flow rivers of living water.’” — John 7:37–38</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-prayer" aria-labelledby="prayer-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section08">08 · Pray</p>
        <h2 id="prayer-title" data-i18n="prayerTitle">A prayer for reopened wells</h2>
        <p data-i18n="prayer1">Lord Jesus, You alone are the true Source of life. Forgive us for allowing anxiety, sin, busyness, false voices, and distractions to bury our access to You. Awaken our hunger and thirst for You again.</p>
        <p data-i18n="prayer2">Give us grace to keep digging through contention, hostility, and accusation. Teach us to speak truth with wisdom, surrender our rights when that is the faithful response, and value Your witness above our pride.</p>
        <p data-i18n="prayer3">Thank You that Your grace is sufficient and spacious. Establish our altar, tent, and well in Your presence. Reconcile those who are divided, form us through prayer and Your Word, and let Your living water flow from us toward our families, church, city, and the nations. In Jesus’ name, Amen.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="takeaway" aria-label="Study takeaway" data-i18n-aria-label="takeawayAria"><span data-i18n="takeawayLabel">Take this with you</span><p data-i18n="takeaway">Jesus is the Source that never runs dry. Clear what has buried the well, keep digging through opposition, and draw from him until his life in you overflows to others.</p></section>

